# Erdős Problem #99 — near-circle full-core diameter-edge density

**Date:** 2026-09-09  
**Repository base:** PR #119 after DG/DC/DS/OW/GF  
**Run type:** exact cross-route asymptotic sharpening  
**Target:** post-ZF `x_B=o(D)`, `n-v=o(D^2)` branch  
**Depends on:** DE exposed-hull train theorem, ZE/ZT exact defect ledger, DG/DC  
**External web search:** not used  
**Computation:** decimal evaluation only; proof is analytic

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The near-circle endpoint and the asymptotically full-core endpoint were previously analyzed separately. Their combination yields a stronger diameter-edge lower bound than either DB or DG+NM.

If a post-ZF survivor sequence satisfies

\[
x_B=o(D)
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
n-v=o(D^2),
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac hD
\ge
\frac\pi2+\frac25\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}
=1.9517214495\ldots .
}
\tag{FC.1}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{2h-q}{D}
\ge
\frac45\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}
=0.7618502455\ldots ,
}
\tag{FC.2}
\]

and, because `x_B=o(D)` and `2h-q=x_B+Q+2s_Z`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{Q+2s_Z}{D}
\ge
\frac45\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}.
}
\tag{FC.3}
\]

This improves DG+NM's `pi/5=0.628318...` defect constant in the full-core branch.

---

## 1. Inputs

Write

\[
\Delta:=\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}.
\tag{1.1}
\]

The full-core hypothesis

\[
n-v=o(D^2)
\tag{1.2}
\]

allows the audited DE/DF asymptotic train theorem to be used. DE gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{T-q}{D}
\ge
c_0:=2\sqrt\Delta-\frac\pi2.
}
\tag{1.3}
\]

Numerically

\[
c_0=0.3338292869\ldots .
\]

The near-circle hypothesis `x_B=o(D)` gives by DG/DC

\[
\boxed{
\frac qD\to\pi.
}
\tag{1.4}
\]

The exact post-ZF identities are

\[
2f=(T-q)+d_H+s_Z,
\tag{1.5}
\]

\[
q=x_B+Q+2d_H+2s_Z,
\tag{1.6}
\]

where

\[
d_H=q-h,
\qquad
Q=\sum_{F\ exc}(\deg F-4),
\qquad
f=\#\{\text{exceptional bounded faces}\}.
\]

Every exceptional face has degree at least five, so

\[
\boxed{Q\ge f.}
\tag{1.7}
\]

---

## 2. Normalize the exact ledger

Pass to an arbitrary subsequence on which

\[
\frac hD\to y,
\qquad
\frac{s_Z}{D}\to s
\]

after passing further if necessary; boundedness follows from `h<=q<=pi D` and `s_Z<=h`.

By (1.4),

\[
\frac{d_H}{D}=\frac{q-h}{D}\to\pi-y.
\tag{2.1}
\]

From (1.5) and DE.1.3,

\[
2\liminf\frac fD
\ge
c_0+(\pi-y)+s.
\tag{2.2}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac fD
\ge
\frac{c_0+\pi-y+s}{2}.
}
\tag{2.3}
\]

On the other hand, divide (1.6) by `D`. Since `x_B/D->0`,

\[
\frac QD
\to
\pi-2(\pi-y)-2s
=2y-\pi-2s
\tag{2.4}
\]

along the chosen subsequence whenever the displayed variables converge; otherwise use limsup/liminf in the same inequality below.

Since `Q>=f`, combine (2.3)-(2.4):

\[
2y-\pi-2s
\ge
\frac{c_0+\pi-y+s}{2}.
\tag{2.5}
\]

Multiply by two and rearrange:

\[
5y
\ge
3\pi+c_0+5s.
\tag{2.6}
\]

Since `s>=0`,

\[
y\ge\frac{3\pi+c_0}{5}.
\tag{2.7}
\]

Now substitute

\[
c_0=2\sqrt\Delta-\frac\pi2:
\]

\[
\frac{3\pi+c_0}{5}
=
\frac{5\pi/2+2\sqrt\Delta}{5}
=
\frac\pi2+\frac25\sqrt\Delta.
\tag{2.8}
\]

This proves FC.1 on every convergent subsequence, hence globally.

---

## 3. Branching and contact-defect constants

DG/DC gives `q/D->pi`. Therefore

\[
\frac{2h-q}{D}
\to 2y-\pi
\]

on every convergent subsequence for `h/D`. Applying FC.1,

\[
\liminf\frac{2h-q}{D}
\ge
2\left(\frac\pi2+\frac25\sqrt\Delta\right)-\pi
=
\frac45\sqrt\Delta.
\]

This proves FC.2.

The exact branching identity is

\[
2h-q=x_B+Q+2s_Z.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Since `x_B=o(D)`, FC.3 follows immediately.

Numerically

\[
\frac45\sqrt\Delta
=0.7618502455\ldots .
\]

---

## 4. A useful strengthened inequality retaining mixed-row surplus

Equation (2.6) actually proves more than FC.1. Along every convergent subsequence,

\[
\boxed{
\frac hD
\ge
\frac\pi2+\frac25\sqrt\Delta
+\frac{s_Z}{D}-o(1).
}
\tag{4.1}
\]

Thus linear extra mixed-framework dependence pushes the diameter-edge density upward one-for-one asymptotically.

Equivalently, because `h/D<=q/D->pi`,

\[
\boxed{
\limsup\frac{s_Z}{D}
\le
\frac\pi2-\frac25\sqrt\Delta
=1.1898712040\ldots .
}
\tag{4.2}
\]

This is not a small upper bound by itself, but it exposes the exact exchange rate between extra mixed stress dependencies and diameter edges in the near-circle full-core phase.

---

## 5. Relation to the previous constants

Universal post-ZF DB:

\[
\liminf h/D\ge1.4781702664\ldots .
\]

DG+NM under `x_B=o(D)`:

\[
\liminf h/D\ge3\pi/5
=1.8849555922\ldots .
\]

FC under the additional asymptotically full-core hypothesis:

\[
\boxed{
\liminf h/D\ge1.9517214495\ldots .
}
\]

The improvement comes from the DE exposed-hull train theorem, not another rearrangement of DB/NM.

---

## 6. Remaining branch split

GF proved that under `x_B=o(D)` either

1. `n-v=o(D^2)`, or
2. there is a bounded convex exceptional face of perimeter `Omega(D)` containing quadratic invisible population.

FC now sharpens the first branch to:

```text
near-circular force hull
+ asymptotically full positive core
    -> h/D >= 1.9517214495...
    -> (Q+2s_Z)/D >= 0.7618502455...
    -> at least c_0 D exposed-hull train excess from DE.
```

Therefore any closing proof may treat the full-core and giant-face branches separately without losing the stronger diameter-density information in the former.

---

## 7. Compact handoff

```text
LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN

Assume post-ZF, x_B=o(D), n-v=o(D^2).
Then DG/DC gives q/D->pi.
DE gives
    liminf(T-q)/D >= c0
    =2sqrt(Delta)-pi/2,
    Delta=pi/(2sqrt3).
ZE gives
    2f=(T-q)+d_H+s_Z.
ZT gives, since x_B=o(D),
    Q/D = pi-2d_H/D-2s_Z/D+o(1).
Also Q>=f.
Writing h/D->y, s_Z/D->s, d_H/D->pi-y gives
    2y-pi-2s >= (c0+pi-y+s)/2,
so
    y >= pi/2+(2/5)sqrt(Delta)+s.
Therefore
    liminf h/D >= 1.9517214495...,
    liminf(2h-q)/D >= (4/5)sqrt(Delta)=0.7618502455...,
    liminf(Q+2s_Z)/D >= 0.7618502455....
```
